A||N||3||Fiero_Freak||3.4 dampener?||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero_Freak||04-12-2001||11:54 PM||||    I know I need to get the balanced flywheel, but what about the dampener.  Which one do I use the 2.8's or the 3.4's.  I heard something about having to mark the dampener that you have to put on.  Does anyone know this process?  thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||04-13-2001||12:19 AM||||There is probably a better way, but I used the 3.4 dampener. I placed the 2.8 dampener over the 3.4, alighned the keyways in the center and used a hacksaw to mark the new timing mark in the 3.4 dampener to match the 2.8's. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_Freak||04-13-2001||08:09 AM||||Cool.. I just didn't have a clue as to what to use.  it'll definately be time to paint the mark that I need. Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||04-13-2001||05:11 PM||||Use the 3.4-liter damper and make sure there are no cracks in the rubber. The absolute best way to get the correct TDC mark on the damper is to use a dial indicator on top of the piston to get TDC. Then you just mark the damper where it lines up with the pointer on the timing cover and your golden. Often the factory marks can be off a little so this is the best way to get it. Cooters way will work but if you want to be absolutely sure that the TDC mark indicates TDC than use this method. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
